00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:56.740
So while you're running errands or just sitting in your driveway trying to do work, it's like

516
00:19:56.740 --> 00:19:58.740
you're getting a gentle rolling massage on your back.

517
00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:01.780
Listen, there are many things we loved about the Envision.

518
00:20:02.020 --> 00:20:02.700
It's zippy.

519
00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:04.100
It's got get up and go.

520
00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:05.160
It looks classy.

521
00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:07.120
But it's also super functional.

522
00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:10.680
And running my usual mom errands, it just felt like a mini vacation.

523
00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:14.660
The all-new Buick Envision was an MVP for us these past few weeks.

524
00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:19.240
Staying motivated and staying on top of what we need is important in making the most of

525
00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:19.880
a busy week.

526
00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:22.360
And the Envision really helped us out here.

527
00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.300
If you're looking for the right vehicle to help you do the same,

528
00:20:25.460 --> 00:20:30.000
check out the Buick Envision at buick.com or at your local Buick dealer.

529
00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:35.510
And we are back.

530
00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:39.210
And now it's time to break down how we make office ladies.

531
00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:43.990
I thought we could kick things off with a question from Harris in Akron, Ohio.

532
00:20:44.330 --> 00:20:45.410
Hello, Harris.

533
00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:46.350
Hello, Harris.

534
00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:47.570
Okay.

535
00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:50.970
Harris says, I never knew how much I needed this podcaSt.

536
00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:54.430
It's been so great, especially this past year.

537
00:20:54.710 --> 00:20:56.430
What inspired you to make it?

538
00:20:56.530 --> 00:20:57.590
And thank you.

539
00:20:58.030 --> 00:20:59.950
I mean, Harris, come on.

540
00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:02.730
Thank you, Harris, for asking.

541
00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:04.950
What a lovely, lovely comment.

542
00:21:05.830 --> 00:21:06.450
Let's see.

543
00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:08.330
What inspired us to make it?

544
00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:12.850
I mean, I think the biggest thing that inspired us to make it was our desire to work together

545
00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:13.270
again.

546
00:21:13.390 --> 00:21:14.090
For sure.

547
00:21:14.330 --> 00:21:14.870
Yeah.

548
00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:15.450
Yeah.

549
00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:17.790
We've wanted to work together ever since the office ended.

550
00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:23.490
Like, literally, the day we drove off the Dunder Mifflin set, we were like, okay, when

551
00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:24.530
are we working together again?

552
00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:26.510
Because we have to have lunch every day.

553
00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:30.770
And I wanted to do your show, Haters Back Off, but I was super pregs and I couldn't travel.

554
00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:31.690
I know.

555
00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:32.310
Remember that?

556
00:21:32.310 --> 00:21:32.830
Yes.

557
00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:35.630
And then you were like, and come be on my show.

558
00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:37.470
We did this back and forth.

559
00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:39.650
We're like, guys, hey, we just got a new job.

560
00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:40.810
Can my friend Jenna come by?

561
00:21:40.910 --> 00:21:41.550
It really was.

562
00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:42.730
Can she be in it?

563
00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:47.910
Is that obnoxious that we would get a job and we'd be like, do you have a role, maybe

564
00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:49.170
a scene I could do with my best friend?

565
00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:51.950
Is there a little quirky blonde lady can cruise through?

566
00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:53.250
Yeah.

567
00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:55.690
So we really just wanted to work together again.

568
00:21:55.690 --> 00:22:00.930
But I would say something else that was happening after the office ended was we realized how

569
00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:06.950
spoiled we were on the office in the sense that the office hours were 6 a.m.

570
00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:07.750
to 6 p.m.

571
00:22:07.870 --> 00:22:09.830
I mean, we have Steve Krell to thank for that, right?

572
00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:10.530
We really do.

573
00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:12.090
He was such a family guy.

574
00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:16.670
Before any of us had families, Steve was the guy that had a family and he wanted to be

575
00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:17.330
home for dinner.

576
00:22:17.570 --> 00:22:18.050
He did.

577
00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:22.170
And so we were able to do this work schedule that was very family friendly.

578
00:22:22.170 --> 00:22:24.010
And it was in one place.

579
00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:27.750
We rarely went on location or had night shoots.

580
00:22:27.930 --> 00:22:30.550
And when we did, it was just fun because it was such a novelty.

581
00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:37.430
But then as we started getting these jobs after the office, there was so much travel

582
00:22:37.430 --> 00:22:37.870
involved.

583
00:22:38.010 --> 00:22:38.970
We were away a lot.

584
00:22:39.110 --> 00:22:44.210
When we would talk about that, we would say, oh, my gosh, in order to work as an actor,

585
00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:48.810
it seems like I have to go away or I have to work these jobs where-

586
00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:49.750
Odd hours.

587
00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:50.470
Odd hours.

588
00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:54.810
And I was missing dinners and I was FaceTiming bedtimes.

589
00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:57.750
And listen, I know this is true in a lot of professions.

590
00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:03.090
You've said before, your sister is a nurse and she'll have these three-day shifts at the

591
00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:05.290
hospital that sort of take her out of commission.

592
00:23:05.490 --> 00:23:05.650
Right.

593
00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:12.430
But as I was watching my kids growing up and thinking about, gosh, I wonder, is there a

594
00:23:12.430 --> 00:23:18.210
way we can be working together but more available to our families and home more?

595
00:23:18.610 --> 00:23:19.130
Yes.

596
00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:22.690
And this whole conversation was happening, by the way, while I was working on splitting

597
00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:24.250
up together a TV show.

598
00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:26.590
And I would talk to Oliver Hudson about it.

599
00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:32.090
And he said one day, he said, well, Jenna, what you and Angela should do is a podcaSt.

600
00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:34.370
You'd be home all the time.

601
00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:35.230
Oh, my God.

602
00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:37.190
Should I send Oliver Hudson a thank you?

603
00:23:37.510 --> 00:23:38.610
You probably should.

604
00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:40.690
I mean, he's who planted the seed.

605
00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:42.630
He 100% planted the seed.

606
00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:43.410
100%.

607
00:23:43.790 --> 00:23:47.250
He was like, Jenna, I know how much you like to wear sweatpants.

608
00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:48.930
I know how much you love your BFF.

609
00:23:49.250 --> 00:23:52.510
I'm telling you, this job is going to tick all the boxes for you.

610
00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:53.890
You should really look into it.

611
00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:58.670
Now, meanwhile, you guys, I had been working on a show in Canada, in Vancouver, which

612
00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:00.290
beautiful place, loved it.

613
00:24:00.390 --> 00:24:03.030
But I was flying back and forth and back and forth and back and forth.

614
00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:10.110
So when Jenna was like, Ange, I have an idea here of how we could work in sweatpants

615
00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:13.190
and be home more, I was like, ding, ding, ding.

616
00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:14.650
Tell me about it.

617
00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:15.510
Yeah.

618
00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:17.690
So that was really the inspiration.

619
00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:20.870
Thank you, Oliver Hudson, for planting the seed.

620
00:24:21.010 --> 00:24:23.930
There will be a gift basket of some kind of muffins headed your way.

621
00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:30.010
Well, next, we have a question from Ben A., who asked, can you explain how a typical podcast

622
00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:31.650
episode gets recorded?

623
00:24:32.090 --> 00:24:34.370
What does a typical week look like for you?

624
00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:36.470
This is actually such a good question.

625
00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:40.930
My kids have asked this, because they get to see part of the process, right?

626
00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:41.270
Yeah.

627
00:24:41.690 --> 00:24:43.290
And they really want to come in the studio.

628
00:24:43.750 --> 00:24:44.670
My kids do, too.

629
00:24:44.770 --> 00:24:45.150
I know.

630
00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:46.610
We need to have, oh, my God.

631
00:24:46.770 --> 00:24:47.230
Family day.

632
00:24:47.270 --> 00:24:48.050
Family day.

633
00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:51.910
Sam and Cassie, we're going to have family day.

634
00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:54.530
So because they just see the part of it at home.

635
00:24:54.890 --> 00:24:55.110
Yeah.

636
00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:55.670
All right.

637
00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:56.550
So let's break it down.

638
00:24:56.950 --> 00:24:57.170
Okay.

639
00:24:57.290 --> 00:25:00.690
So, Angela, how do you start your week when you plan for the podcast?

640
00:25:01.090 --> 00:25:05.030
Well, first of all, I watch the episode we're breaking down like three or four times.

641
00:25:05.230 --> 00:25:05.490
Same.

642
00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:05.970
Right?

643
00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:06.450
Yes.

644
00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:10.670
So first, I watch it just like as an audience member.

645
00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:14.330
And the kids usually watch it with me, because my kids, you know, are older, and they're really

646
00:25:14.330 --> 00:25:14.750
into it.

647
00:25:14.830 --> 00:25:15.730
So I watch it with them.

648
00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:18.930
It's so interesting to see the things they love, you know?

649
00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:22.770
And then I'll watch it again, and I have my outline.

650
00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:27.850
We have an outline, you guys, of the episode, like a scene-by-scene outline that we print out.

651
00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:28.490
Yes.

652
00:25:28.630 --> 00:25:31.930
Our associate producer, Ainsley Bubico, puts that together for us.

653
00:25:32.090 --> 00:25:32.870
Thank you, Ainsley.

654
00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:36.070
So my second pass at watching it, I watch it with that outline.

655
00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:38.550
And I go through, and I just start making notes.

656
00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:41.170
You guys, my notes used to be on note cards.

657
00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:46.170
But Angela has graduated everybody to adulthood.

658
00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:47.290
Oh, now, listen.

659
00:25:47.530 --> 00:25:49.930
And she uses a proper outline.

660
00:25:50.150 --> 00:25:51.230
Applause for you, lady.

661
00:25:51.250 --> 00:25:51.550
I know.

662
00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:53.630
I put it all in one Word document now.

663
00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:59.250
But I did that because I was in the closet, my husband's closet, podcasting for a year.

664
00:25:59.290 --> 00:26:00.830
And I had no room for my note cards.

665
00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:01.210
I know.

666
00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:05.450
I still feel like I missed the note cards, if I'm honest with you.

667
00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:07.470
Well, back in studio, they're coming back.

668
00:26:07.470 --> 00:26:08.190
So get ready.

669
00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:08.570
All right.

670
00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:10.830
But anyway, so then I start making notes.

671
00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:12.510
Then we start really doing research.

672
00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:17.370
Anything that's mentioned in the episode that I'm curious about, I'll start, you know, Googling

673
00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:20.250
it and looking it up and finding out information.

674
00:26:20.250 --> 00:26:22.910
We'll reach out to cast and crew with questions.

675
00:26:23.450 --> 00:26:27.990
Then I personally take the DVD commentary, Deleted Scenes and Bloopers.

676
00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:28.570
Right, Jenna?

677
00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:29.010
Yep.

678
00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:32.630
And you really go into fan questions.

679
00:26:33.070 --> 00:26:33.370
Yes.

680
00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:35.310
And that's also with help from Ainsley.

681
00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:40.370
She'll pull questions from our mailbag, from our Instagram, and she sends them to me.

682
00:26:40.550 --> 00:26:43.550
And then I go through and try to figure out what do we know the answer to.

683
00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:44.030
Yeah.

684
00:26:44.150 --> 00:26:46.710
And we will trade off texting cast and crew.

685
00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:48.070
So Jenna will reach out to people.

686
00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:49.090
I reach out to people.

687
00:26:49.090 --> 00:26:55.530
I mean, we just couldn't do the podcast without the love and support of The Office family.

688
00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:56.350
It's true.

689
00:26:56.610 --> 00:27:00.610
And, you know, in this whole process, because mine is very similar to yours, Angela, the way

690
00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:05.830
I watch it, we try not to tell each other specifically what we're doing.

691
00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:07.550
Because I want to surprise Angela.

692
00:27:07.730 --> 00:27:10.950
Like, I don't always tell her my fast facts.

693
00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:13.210
I don't always tell her my deep dives.

694
00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:14.030
Same, same.

695
00:27:14.350 --> 00:27:19.070
Like, if there is a nugget in the DVD commentary are my favorite, a deleted scene that

696
00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:22.250
I know is going to rock your world, I won't tell you.

697
00:27:22.390 --> 00:27:25.830
I will give the audio clips to Sam and let him play it.

698
00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:27.270
And you react in the moment.

699
00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:33.110
So after all of this prep, we record our episodes every Thursday.

700
00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:34.010
Mm-hmm.

701
00:27:34.370 --> 00:27:37.570
And then we edit the following week.

702
00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:45.150
So our producer, Cassie Jerkins, and our sound engineer, Sam Kiefer, they will put together

703
00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:45.570
the episode.

704
00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:46.850
They assemble it for us.

705
00:27:46.910 --> 00:27:47.090
Mm-hmm.

706
00:27:47.290 --> 00:27:48.770
And then we get to listen to it.

707
00:27:48.850 --> 00:27:49.670
And then we give notes.

708
00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:53.010
This is going to be weird to say, you guys, I love listening to the podcaSt.

709
00:27:54.050 --> 00:27:54.790
I love it.

710
00:27:55.670 --> 00:27:56.310
We do.

711
00:27:56.410 --> 00:27:57.350
We do, you guys.

712
00:27:57.550 --> 00:27:58.010
I will.

713
00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:00.450
This is like such a lame story.

714
00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:02.870
But I listen to it while I do chores.

715
00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:03.770
You do too, Jenna.

716
00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:04.330
Oh, yeah.

717
00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:07.550
So one of my favorite things is to listen to it while I fold clothes.

718
00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:10.690
And Josh walked in, my husband, and I was just smiling.

719
00:28:10.690 --> 00:28:12.330
I was just grinning ear to ear.

720
00:28:12.430 --> 00:28:13.870
And he was like, what are you listening to?

721
00:28:14.010 --> 00:28:15.170
And I was like, oh, me and Jenna.

722
00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:18.510
He was like, oh, my gosh, you're such a dork.

723
00:28:19.870 --> 00:28:22.550
Well, it's like I get to hang out with you for a whole hour.

724
00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:28.130
Yeah, and so after we have listened to ourselves and been delighted by ourselves and tackled

725
00:28:28.130 --> 00:28:33.090
big chores, because Jenna, you have told me you'll save a big chore in the house.

726
00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:35.210
You're going to organize your pantry.

727
00:28:35.590 --> 00:28:39.850
Oh, I organized my son's closet while I listened to Dinner Party, because I was like, that's

728
00:28:39.850 --> 00:28:40.470
a long one.

729
00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:42.570
That's a good closet cleaning chore.

730
00:28:42.610 --> 00:28:43.590
I can get a big project done.

731
00:28:43.670 --> 00:28:43.990
Exactly.

732
00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:51.530
So then we have a Zoom with Sam and Cassie, where we go through our notes together.

733
00:28:51.530 --> 00:28:55.230
And we discuss things we want to cut out, things we want to keep.

734
00:28:55.470 --> 00:28:59.870
And guys, I know, I've seen comments that are like, why do you edit anything?

735
00:28:59.890 --> 00:29:01.230
I want to listen to it.

736
00:29:01.330 --> 00:29:01.850
You don't.

737
00:29:01.930 --> 00:29:02.310
You don't.

738
00:29:02.370 --> 00:29:02.830
Oh, my gosh.

739
00:29:02.970 --> 00:29:06.590
First of all, sometimes we're recording, and I'm like, I'm really hungry, so I start

740
00:29:06.590 --> 00:29:07.510
eating a granola bar.

741
00:29:07.630 --> 00:29:08.690
You don't need to hear that.

742
00:29:08.790 --> 00:29:09.610
You don't need to hear that.

743
00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:11.610
We need to edit out meat crunching.

744
00:29:11.730 --> 00:29:14.310
I mean, we've been known to go on some tangents.

745
00:29:14.450 --> 00:29:16.250
Oh, real long tangents.

746
00:29:16.510 --> 00:29:17.850
Can you believe it, guys?

747
00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:19.370
Are you shocked?

748
00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:26.130
And then also, sometimes there'll be part of the episode that we both separately loved,

749
00:29:26.150 --> 00:29:29.130
and we both bring similar information.

750
00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:34.730
So then we decide, okay, wait, Jenna, I kind of like your spin on that, or I like what you

751
00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:35.250
research.

752
00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:36.270
You lead with that.

753
00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:37.110
I'll take this.

754
00:29:37.330 --> 00:29:40.350
Sometimes we're sorting it out right then and there.

755
00:29:40.770 --> 00:29:40.950
Yeah.

756
00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:43.030
Well, that's kind of how we do it.

757
00:29:43.030 --> 00:29:48.270
I would say, overall, we spend about 20 to 25 hours per week working on the podcaSt.

758
00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:50.550
It's a full-time, part-time job.

759
00:29:52.310 --> 00:29:55.930
And that's kind of the long and long of it, I think, right?

760
00:29:56.330 --> 00:29:57.810
Ben, did we go on too long?

761
00:29:57.970 --> 00:29:58.670
So sorry, Ben.

762
00:29:58.950 --> 00:29:59.630
Sorry, Ben.

763
00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:01.690
And I guess I do want to say one more thing.

764
00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:06.270
I want to give a shout out to our executive producer, Cody Fisher, because we just couldn't

765
00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:10.510
do this without our team and our partnership with Earwolf.

766
00:30:10.710 --> 00:30:11.810
We're very grateful.

767
00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:17.670
And then I just want to say that one of the things that has gotten me through this past

768
00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:19.810
year has been our team at Earwolf.

769
00:30:19.950 --> 00:30:22.010
It has been our weekly edit sessions.

770
00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:27.410
Not only do I enjoy doing the podcast with Jenna, but I equally enjoy hanging out with

771
00:30:27.410 --> 00:30:28.990
Sam and Cassie over a Zoom.

772
00:30:29.390 --> 00:30:34.850
We always have some hot topic we have to chitty chat about at the top before we get to work.

773
00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:41.330
And just seeing their faces, even though it's been on a TV, has just, I don't know.

774
00:30:41.410 --> 00:30:42.470
I just look forward to it.

775
00:30:43.030 --> 00:30:47.810
Well, this leads me very well into our next question, Angela, from Natasha B.

776
00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:52.430
What would you say is the most challenging aspect of making the podcast each week?

777
00:30:52.550 --> 00:30:55.530
Does working with your best friend make things easier or harder?

778
00:30:55.890 --> 00:31:00.750
Like, do you waste time in meetings just chatting as friends?

779
00:31:01.830 --> 00:31:03.990
Well, okay, okay, Natasha.

780
00:31:04.270 --> 00:31:08.310
I definitely think being best friends makes it easier in so many ways, right?

781
00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:08.850
Yeah, I do.

782
00:31:08.990 --> 00:31:12.970
We're both really efficient, and I don't think we waste each other's time.

783
00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:17.290
Although we, yeah, yeah, we do a lot of gabbing on the Zoom.

784
00:31:17.290 --> 00:31:19.470
It's more of a question for Cassie and Sam, maybe.

785
00:31:19.470 --> 00:31:21.490
Do we gab too much?

786
00:31:21.590 --> 00:31:22.210
Do we gab too much, Sam?

787
00:31:22.550 --> 00:31:24.310
I think it's an accurate amount.

788
00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:25.810
I think it's just the right amount.

789
00:31:26.330 --> 00:31:27.630
An accurate amount.

790
00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:29.510
That's an interesting choice of word.

791
00:31:30.730 --> 00:31:31.510
Well, Sam.

792
00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:32.410
I think it's a healthy amount.

793
00:31:32.690 --> 00:31:33.410
Healthy amount.

794
00:31:33.610 --> 00:31:35.790
You also have really good stories, too.

795
00:31:36.270 --> 00:31:40.050
Yeah, I think I'm okay with it because I contribute so much to it.

796
00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:41.630
Maybe this is a question for Cassie.

797
00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:42.030
Yeah, exactly.

798
00:31:42.470 --> 00:31:42.950
Cassie.

799
00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:43.430
Like on a Zoom.

800
00:31:43.610 --> 00:31:44.030
I know.

801
00:31:44.750 --> 00:31:45.390
All right.

802
00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:47.410
I agree that we both have a good work ethic.

803
00:31:47.610 --> 00:31:48.970
We're both very driven people.

804
00:31:49.210 --> 00:31:50.890
What would you say is the most challenging?

805
00:31:53.120 --> 00:31:54.320
About doing the podcast?

806
00:31:54.460 --> 00:31:54.640
Yeah.

807
00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:55.380
The most challenging.

808
00:31:57.460 --> 00:32:01.560
I think for me, it's just that I want to make sure we get it right.

809
00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:09.980
The Office is this hugely popular, beloved show to so many people.

810
00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:13.000
And the truth is, the fans, they know the show.

811
00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:14.420
You guys know the show.

812
00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:15.920
Better than anyone.

813
00:32:16.240 --> 00:32:16.290
Yeah.

814
00:32:16.660 --> 00:32:20.420
And every week, I just want Jenna and I to do right by you guys.

815
00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:22.860
And I also want to do right by the cast and crew.

816
00:32:23.340 --> 00:32:27.620
I mean, hundreds of people work together every week to make The Office come to life.

817
00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:31.160
And each and every one of them were an integral part of the show's success.

818
00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:33.100
And Jenna and I talk about this all the time.

819
00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:34.600
We just want everyone represented.

820
00:32:35.140 --> 00:32:39.060
We want to be able to share all the stories, all the little tidbits.

821
00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:44.620
And I think the pressure I feel is I don't want to let the show's legacy down, you know?

822
00:32:44.800 --> 00:32:45.000
Yeah.

823
00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:46.360
And we do miss things.

824
00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:47.180
We do.

825
00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:47.800
Yeah.

826
00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:48.880
We get things wrong.

827
00:32:49.020 --> 00:32:49.940
We get it wrong.

828
00:32:50.120 --> 00:32:55.080
And I really love our office ladies, listeners, this community of people.

829
00:32:55.200 --> 00:32:56.660
You guys are so kind.

830
00:32:56.660 --> 00:33:00.720
And you will lovingly let us know when we got it wrong.

831
00:33:00.860 --> 00:33:04.100
And that's the whole reason we started doing the revisited episodes.

832
00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:04.760
Yeah.

833
00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:11.760
For me, the most challenging aspect of making the podcast is probably just what's most challenging

834
00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:14.340
for me in general in my life.

835
00:33:14.840 --> 00:33:15.960
I'm going to share.

836
00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:16.500
Yeah.

837
00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:20.180
Is just managing my anxiety and nervousness.

838
00:33:20.380 --> 00:33:25.420
It was especially hard for me as an actress because sets are a very social environment.

839
00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:31.440
I think sometimes people assume that all actors are just extroverts and that we have this natural

840
00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:35.820
ease with being in the spotlight, but that is definitely not the case for me.

841
00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:40.900
So in a lot of ways, the podcast is a much better fit for me because our team is small.

842
00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:42.640
I do better in small groups of people.

843
00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:45.680
But even still, it is a challenge.

844
00:33:46.140 --> 00:33:48.400
Well, you've shared a lot with me about this over the years.

845
00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:53.540
And you have a really great analogy that I thought was so helpful.

846
00:33:53.900 --> 00:33:55.740
You call it your backpack analogy.

847
00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:59.380
And I want you to share it because, Jenna, I think it's relatable.

848
00:33:59.380 --> 00:34:03.240
And I've shared it with my kids because we all have stress and anxiety.

849
00:34:03.500 --> 00:34:04.900
It's something we all deal with.

850
00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:06.680
It manifests differently for everyone.

851
00:34:06.940 --> 00:34:10.500
But this is a great way to explain your stress.

852
00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:11.460
Will you tell us about it?

853
00:34:11.680 --> 00:34:11.940
Yes.

854
00:34:12.140 --> 00:34:13.420
My backpack analogy.

855
00:34:13.640 --> 00:34:19.440
The way I think about it is my anxiety is a backpack that I wear every single day.

856
00:34:19.780 --> 00:34:21.540
I take it with me every time.

857
00:34:21.700 --> 00:34:25.320
But some days the backpack is heavy and some days it's light.

858
00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:29.780
And some days it really weighs me down and makes it super hard to walk through my day.

859
00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:31.440
And sometimes I barely notice it.

860
00:34:31.720 --> 00:34:32.960
But it's always there.

861
00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:34.600
And I'm never going to get rid of it.

862
00:34:35.200 --> 00:34:41.060
What I love about our friendship and getting to work with someone who knows me so intimately

863
00:34:41.060 --> 00:34:45.460
is you have said to me, lady, how heavy is the backpack today?

864
00:34:46.460 --> 00:34:47.760
And then I can tell you.

865
00:34:47.960 --> 00:34:51.840
And then I have different strategies for managing a heavy backpack.

866
00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:56.400
But sometimes, man, the heavy backpack, you wake up, there's no reason for it.

867
00:34:56.480 --> 00:34:58.400
You're just like, man, it's a heavy backpack day?

868
00:34:58.500 --> 00:34:59.360
You've got to be kidding me.

869
00:34:59.440 --> 00:34:59.980
Come on.

870
00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:00.600
Right.

871
00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:01.980
And I hate that.

872
00:35:02.300 --> 00:35:04.780
But, you know, that's what it is.

873
00:35:04.940 --> 00:35:07.820
I'm no longer looking to get rid of the backpack.

874
00:35:07.820 --> 00:35:09.540
And I don't expect to.

875
00:35:09.760 --> 00:35:15.820
And just that little shift of acknowledging that it's always there has really helped me a lot.

876
00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:16.380
Yeah.

877
00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:17.500
It's just me.

878
00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:18.920
And it comes with me.

879
00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:19.600
Right.

880
00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:21.060
And that you're going to be okay.

881
00:35:21.500 --> 00:35:21.840
Yes.

882
00:35:22.140 --> 00:35:24.020
Well, I love you and your backpack.

883
00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:25.380
Well, thanks, lady.

884
00:35:26.880 --> 00:35:27.400
Thanks.

885
00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:29.480
You help me carry my backpack a lot.

886
00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:29.900
Aw.

887
00:35:30.340 --> 00:35:35.140
Well, since we're talking about our friendship and all the ways it relates back to the podcast,

888
00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:36.560
Jenna, I have a fan question.

889
00:35:36.740 --> 00:35:37.080
Okay.

890
00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:38.360
From Molly S.

891
00:35:38.840 --> 00:35:42.060
How has your friendship changed while making this podcast?

892
00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:45.160
Have you found out new things about each other?

893
00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:45.900
Hmm.

894
00:35:47.060 --> 00:35:48.080
Well, yeah.

895
00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:51.780
Well, first of all, I had no idea Angela loved no cards so much.

896
00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:52.680
I really didn't.

897
00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:56.200
Angela also wears lipstick to every Zoom.

898
00:35:56.640 --> 00:35:58.300
Like, she will not do her hair.

899
00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:01.900
She might be in a bathrobe, but she put on lipstick.

900
00:36:01.900 --> 00:36:03.200
I find that hilarious.

901
00:36:04.700 --> 00:36:06.040
Well, here's the thing.

902
00:36:06.660 --> 00:36:08.320
Jenna always has great hair.

903
00:36:08.520 --> 00:36:09.600
Like, I don't get it.

904
00:36:09.740 --> 00:36:13.340
Like, almost every morning for our Zooms, she blows out her hair.

905
00:36:13.480 --> 00:36:14.620
Like, this beautiful blowout.

906
00:36:14.620 --> 00:36:17.760
Today, I am looking at her, and she has a beautiful blowout.

907
00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:20.220
But she never wears lipstick.

908
00:36:20.420 --> 00:36:20.600
Nope.

909
00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:21.180
Never.

910
00:36:21.380 --> 00:36:26.220
I can't tell you how many interviews, press junkets, red carpets we have done.

911
00:36:26.220 --> 00:36:28.480
We're about to step out to have our photo taken.

912
00:36:28.500 --> 00:36:30.720
And I'll be like, lady, you have no lipstick on.

913
00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:31.200
Yeah.

914
00:36:31.360 --> 00:36:32.180
I hate lipstick.

915
00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:37.860
And I don't feel, like, presentable unless I have lipstick on, which makes me feel like

916
00:36:37.860 --> 00:36:40.220
between the two of us, we're almost a fully dressed person.

917
00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:41.340
This is true.

918
00:36:41.520 --> 00:36:41.680
Right?

919
00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:44.400
I need my hair done, and then I feel done.

920
00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:45.040
Oh, no.

921
00:36:45.120 --> 00:36:47.400
I throw it in a ponytail, but I put on my lips.

922
00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:51.540
Well, lady, I have a quiz for us.

923
00:36:51.680 --> 00:36:52.000
Okay.

924
00:36:52.220 --> 00:36:54.900
This is a best friends quiz that I found online.

925
00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:58.440
It was on Hello Giggles, and it has, like, 40 questions.

926
00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:00.480
I'm not going to ask you 40 questions.

927
00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:01.000
Oh, my gosh.

928
00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:05.420
I just pulled a few that were fun, but I thought this would be fun to try and see.

929
00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:09.560
Maybe we'll see how well we know each other, and maybe we'll even learn something new now.

930
00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:10.180
Are you ready?

931
00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:11.620
Is this the end of our friendship?

932
00:37:11.820 --> 00:37:12.240
I'm kidding.

933
00:37:12.360 --> 00:37:12.920
I hope not.

934
00:37:13.060 --> 00:37:13.560
I'm kidding.

935
00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:14.860
If it is, you're hearing it now.

936
00:37:14.960 --> 00:37:15.480
I'm kidding.

937
00:37:15.620 --> 00:37:15.800
Okay.

938
00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:16.440
Yes, I'm ready.

939
00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:16.760
Okay.

940
00:37:16.800 --> 00:37:17.640
No, it's fun.

941
00:37:17.800 --> 00:37:18.400
Number one.

942
00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:18.680
Okay.

943
00:37:19.540 --> 00:37:23.460
Which game show or reality show would I do best on?

944
00:37:24.000 --> 00:37:25.740
Or you can answer this question.

945
00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:29.680
If I were stuck on a desert island, why would I be an asset?

946
00:37:29.680 --> 00:37:30.500
Okay.

947
00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:38.200
Lady, you would be great on reality TV, but you'd also get kicked off too quickly.

948
00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:43.980
So you're basically saying I would make great TV.

949
00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:44.500
Oh, yeah.

950
00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:45.920
Because I'd be a hot mess.

951
00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:51.340
We'd all want to watch you, but I feel like you would tell someone off or something.

952
00:37:51.740 --> 00:37:53.680
You would want to change the rules.

953
00:37:53.900 --> 00:37:55.020
You'd get kicked off.

954
00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:56.960
They'd vote you off or you'd get kicked off.

955
00:37:57.340 --> 00:38:00.520
And we'd be sad to see you go because you would have been the best thing about it.

956
00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:04.880
And so I'm going to pivot to the desert island question.

957
00:38:05.100 --> 00:38:05.440
Okay.

958
00:38:05.580 --> 00:38:05.800
Okay.

959
00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:10.500
I think you'd be an asset because you're very smart and you're a survivaliSt.

960
00:38:10.620 --> 00:38:11.280
You really are.

961
00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:14.600
And you remember the most random trivia.

962
00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:17.480
Like, you would be the person who would be like, oh, wait, wait, I know this.

963
00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:19.520
I know how to collect rain, water in a jungle.

964
00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:20.540
I know how to do this.

965
00:38:20.800 --> 00:38:25.380
Thank you for saying that because, you know, my family is a really big fan of the show Survivor.

966
00:38:25.480 --> 00:38:25.920
I know.

967
00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:29.020
And I always see them get their chickens, you know,

968
00:38:29.120 --> 00:38:36.380
and I remember this one time they were having this big debate about whether or not they should save the eggs or if they should hard boil them and save them.

969
00:38:36.460 --> 00:38:43.440
And I just wanted to scream at the TV, no, fresh laid eggs can go days unrefrigerated.

970
00:38:43.620 --> 00:38:47.420
But the minute you boil it, you have to eat it, y'all, or you have to refrigerate it.

971
00:38:47.540 --> 00:38:48.620
Do not boil those eggs.

972
00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:49.640
Do not boil them.

973
00:38:49.800 --> 00:38:51.880
This is why I think you'd be good on the island.

974
00:38:52.220 --> 00:38:52.880
Thank you.

975
00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:59.040
But not good on Survivor because, like, the first week you'd be like, I'm sorry, but that is highly inappropriate.

976
00:38:59.820 --> 00:39:01.260
I would have a very hard time.

977
00:39:01.280 --> 00:39:03.480
And I think this challenge is demeaning.

978
00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:11.400
Well, it's funny you say this because my family has actually been rewatching Survivor together.

979
00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:13.020
My kids are super into it.

980
00:39:13.100 --> 00:39:18.660
And my answer was going to be that I think you would be amazing on Survivor.

981
00:39:18.880 --> 00:39:20.300
I think you could win it all.

982
00:39:20.440 --> 00:39:20.880
Why?

983
00:39:20.880 --> 00:39:24.440
Because you are a hard worker and you don't complain.

984
00:39:25.340 --> 00:39:26.020
That's why.

985
00:39:26.200 --> 00:39:31.100
Now, listen, we both have hard worker in common, but that second one on the list would get you booted.

986
00:39:31.380 --> 00:39:31.860
Exactly.

987
00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:34.160
But you would be very friendly.

988
00:39:34.220 --> 00:39:37.560
You would get to know people on, like, this really personal level.

989
00:39:37.800 --> 00:39:39.080
You would be social.

990
00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:43.300
But you also would not be perceived as a physical threat.

991
00:39:43.300 --> 00:39:48.400
But even though I think you would do really well in a lot of the challenges, I think you could fly under the radar.

992
00:39:48.860 --> 00:39:49.840
You would be likable.

993
00:39:50.360 --> 00:39:51.400
I think you could win it all.

994
00:39:52.020 --> 00:39:52.840
Thank you, Jenna.

995
00:39:54.280 --> 00:39:54.640
All right.

996
00:39:54.740 --> 00:39:54.800
Thank you.

997
00:39:54.940 --> 00:39:59.400
I never want to do it because they don't show where they go to the bathroom and I don't want to poop in the woods.

998
00:39:59.600 --> 00:39:59.880
So.

999
00:40:00.020 --> 00:40:01.900
Yeah, they go to the bathroom in a hole in the ground, I think.

1000
00:40:01.900 --> 00:40:02.740
Yeah, I'm out.

1001
00:40:03.720 --> 00:40:05.160
Are you ready for the next question?

1002
00:40:05.260 --> 00:40:05.460
Yes.

1003
00:40:05.880 --> 00:40:09.500
If I became president, what do you think I'd do first?

1004
00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:12.280
Jenna, you would do so many things.

1005
00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:14.180
You would be such a great president.

1006
00:40:14.400 --> 00:40:15.240
Hey, thanks.

1007
00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:16.180
You really would.

1008
00:40:16.480 --> 00:40:18.680
First of all, you're very fair.

1009
00:40:19.060 --> 00:40:22.620
And so I feel like you would really be there for everybody.

1010
00:40:23.240 --> 00:40:30.240
And I know for sure you would make sure men and women get paid maternity leave as part of their job.

1011
00:40:30.360 --> 00:40:30.860
I would.

1012
00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:35.300
And of course, come on, equal pay for equal pockets.

1013
00:40:35.620 --> 00:40:37.420
That would be my first executive order.

1014
00:40:37.660 --> 00:40:37.900
Yeah.

1015
00:40:38.960 --> 00:40:39.720
All right.

1016
00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:46.120
Well, I think you would learn every single name of every single person who works in the White House.

1017
00:40:46.380 --> 00:40:46.940
I would.

1018
00:40:47.120 --> 00:40:49.440
